### XML files (`.xml`):

The `<?xml ... ?>` declaration has to stay the first thing in the document for
it to be well formed, so the header follows it. The block is marked with a `+`
on the opening and closing delimiter and a `|` on every line, which is the style
used by the XML files in this repository:

```
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--+
    |
    | <meta:header>
    |   <meta:licence>
    |     Copyright (C) <year> <copyright holder>.
    |
    |     This information is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify
    |     it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by
    |     the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or
    |     (at your option) any later version.
    |
    |     This information is distributed in the hope that it will be useful,
    |     but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of
    |     MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.  See the
    |     GNU General Public License for more details.
    |
    |     You should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License
    |     along with this program.  If not, see <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.
    |   </meta:licence>
    | </meta:header>
    |
    | AIMetrics: []
    |
    +-->
```

Rules:

- The copyright year must be the current year at the time the file is created.
- The `AIMetrics` block starts as `[]` and is populated by the `ai-metrics` rule.
- The header must be the very first content in the file (before any `package` or
  `import` statements, or any `"""` docstring) — with three exceptions, each of
  which must stay first for the file to work: a shebang line, YAML frontmatter,
  and the `<?xml ... ?>` declaration in an XML file.
- Do not add a licence header if the file already has a `<meta:licence>` block.

### Exempt files

A licence header asserts copyright over the project's authored work. Files that
are not authored work, or that a tool owns, carry no header:

- **Dotfiles** — any file whose name begins with `.`, at any level. For example
  `.gitignore`, `.gitattributes`, `.editorconfig`, `.env`, `.dockerignore`.
- **Lockfiles and machine-generated files** — for example `package-lock.json` or
  `pnpm-lock.yaml`, which are regenerated by their tool rather than written.
- **`agents/rules/*.mdc`** — rule definitions, which start with YAML frontmatter
  that has to stay the first content in the file.

Hand-authored configuration is **not** exempt: YAML, JSON, XML, Dockerfiles and
shell scripts are project source and get a header, using the matching template
above. A workflow file under `.github/workflows/` is authored work and gets one
too — it is configuration with a `#` template, not a dotfile.
